**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 21 in full with exercises; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.112, Fall 2005](../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG), Lecture 36 and its prerequisite context; [Atomic weights, isotopes, and the periodic table — CIAAW and IUPAC](../sources/Atomic-Weights-and-Isotopes.lingua#r_1HF97SGP3GF6YJDRH0D81PQ3S3), nuclide, isotope, and radioactive-element notes.

Nuclear reactions change nuclides and may change elemental identity; ordinary chemical reactions reorganize electronic/chemical states while preserving nuclei under their usual model. Conserve the relevant nuclear counts, charge, and energy with the appropriate reaction description. Mass-number bookkeeping and measured nuclear masses have different roles.

Radioactive decay uses a statistical model. For a simple isolated parent with constant decay probability and no replenishment, expected population follows N(t) = N₀exp(−λt), with half-life ln 2/λ. The same exponential mathematics as first-order kinetics does not make the underlying physical process identical. Decay chains, branching, detection efficiency, and background require additional models.

Distinguish activity as decay rate from amount, absorbed energy/dose quantities, and detector counts. A count-rate observation needs efficiency, geometry, background, timing, and decay/specimen information before it supports a quantitative inference. Nuclear handling and measurements require the actual authorized facilities and trained supervision.

**Work:** balance selected textbook decay/reaction equations, derive the simple decay relation, and reconstruct an identified published-data age or activity calculation with its assumptions. Explain how contamination, an open boundary, a decay chain, or an uncertain initial state would change that inference. Use data and calculations without acquiring or handling radioactive material.

Read the entire nuclear chapter and MIT's application lecture. Nuclear physics, radiochemistry, medicine, and energy engineering retain their deeper specialist studies; this Record supplies the general chemical language and measurement distinctions necessary to approach them.

